Alright, I need to tighten up the agent's verification logic. The recent edits to `agent.ts` added two new state variables: `failureNudges` and `lastBashFailed`. Those will let the agent track when a Bash command fails and later nudge the user to address the failure. However, simply declaring those variables isn’t enough – I have to actually set them during execution.

Looking at the current code around Bash handling, I see this block:

```ts
if (result.status === "ok" && call.tool === "bash") {
  lastVerifyRound = round;
}
```

This only updates `lastVerifyRound` when a Bash command succeeds (`status === "ok"`). When a Bash command fails (`status === "error"`), nothing is recorded, so `lastBashFailed` remains at its default `false`. Consequently, any later logic that checks `lastBashFailed` to decide whether to issue a failure nudge will never fire, even though a Bash error actually occurred. That defeats the purpose of the new `failureNudges` counter.

I need to modify this snippet so that:
1. It always records whether the Bash command failed, by setting `lastBashFailed` based on the result status.
2. It only updates `lastVerifyRound` on successful Bash runs, preserving the existing behavior.
3. The change should be minimal and not disrupt other logic, so I’ll keep the surrounding condition structure but expand it.

The revised block should look like:

```ts
if (call.tool === "bash") {
  lastBashFailed = result.status === "error";
  if (result.status === "ok") lastVerifyRound = round;
}
```

This way, every Bash execution updates `lastBashFailed` appropriately, and `lastVerifyRound` still only moves forward on success. The rest of the verification guard (which checks `lastModifyRound`, `lastVerifyRound`, and `verifyNudges`) can now rely on `lastBashFailed` to decide whether to issue a failure nudge in later passes.

I’ll apply this edit using the `Edit` tool, targeting the exact old string and replacing it with the new block. This aligns with the recent additions and should enable the agent to correctly nudge on Bash failures.

